/** @mainpage Multi-dependency Example Test Suite

@section mde_overview Overview

This suite tests the
<a href="https://example.invalid/multi-dependency-example">multi-dependency-example</a>:
eight small projects in C++, Rust, Go, C# and Python wired into two dependency
cycles. In the native @b ring each stage exports a C ABI and calls the next
language, passing a value on until a hop counter runs out. In the @b web tier
three HTTP services do the same over the network. A value that enters either
cycle must come back having visited every stage.

The example ships as a corpus for review tools, with one trivial always-true
defect injected into every source file. The defect short-circuits each stage,
so a value stops after the very first one. These tests are written against the
@e correct behaviour, so on the as-shipped example they fail with stable
known-issue verdicts recorded in the TRC database, and pass once the defects
are fixed.

@section mde_groups Groups

- @ref mde-ring  - run cpp-demo / rust-demo / the Python demo and count the
                   stages a value actually travels through.
- @ref mde-web   - start the three HTTP services and walk the web cycle with a
                   single request.
- @ref mde-graph - check that the libraries are linked into the cycle the way
                   the build intends (no running code involved).

@section mde_running Running

The suite drives an already-built example. Point @c TE_TS_MDE_SRC at its
checkout (a sibling by default) and build it first with its own @c ./build.sh.
When the example is missing or unbuilt, every test skips rather than fails.

*/
